ZTEXT001000029369f2677e60000000
Zero OS 0.13 - original Zero development system, browser and graphical editor

The implementation is written in Zero. Ezero is the easier language built on
Zero; it provides inferred values, functions, managed memory and one-based
lists. The compiler compiles its own source and emits machine code directly.
There is no C, assembly, Python, shell build script or imported compiler
backend in the project sources.

This is an experimental system with explicit limits. It is not a replacement
for Windows or Linux on arbitrary hardware, and it is not described as bug-free.

Own formats

Documentation and textual fixtures use .ztext, with the ZTEXT001 header,
payload length, checksum and validated UTF-8. This is an encoded container,
not a renamed .txt file. The original reader and writer are tools/text.zero.

  zero-text read README.ztext
  zero-text verify README.ztext
  zero-text make INPUT NEW_DOCUMENT.ztext

Linux x86-64 and Windows x64 versions use the documented host interfaces
directly. They do not invoke another program. Existing output files are
preserved. The native text payload limit is one MiB.

Zero OS's terminal command read detects ZTEXT001 or a .ztext name, validates
the header and checksum before displaying content, and reports unsupported
characters. Its current ASCII terminal and 400-byte files limit .ztext to
368 payload bytes. The native readers support full valid UTF-8.

Images use .zpix with the ZPIX0001 header and original RGB run encoding.
The encoder, verifier and decoder are tools/picture.zero. Specifications and
limits are in tools/picture.ztext. PPM/RGB import or export is an explicit
compatibility operation; those formats are not internal project assets.

Executable seeds use the original ZEROAPP1 application container. ZeroFS is
our own persistent filesystem; data/storage.zdrive preserves the original
raw ZeroFS disk byte for byte. Own archives use .zpack and the original
tools/archive.zero packer/reader; see tools/archive.ztext. They support
checked relative paths, content checksums and explicit executable flags.
No audio or video codec is claimed.

Compatibility is explicit: Linux runs ELF executables, Windows runs PE, and
BIOS/UEFI optical boot uses ISO/El Torito plus a FAT firmware image. Original
Zero code creates these exports. Those external formats are not our inventions.
External firmware and virtual machines used for testing are not project code.

Building and checking

  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_COMPILER.zero --ezero
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_READER.zero --browser
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY

The native builder executes original compiler applications within its own
process. It checks that two compiler generations reproduce identical bytes,
then builds the shared kernel, BIOS/UEFI loaders, tools and optical ISO.
The supplied bootstrap/zero64.zapp is a previous Zero-built compiler seed.
The historical assembly bootstrap is acknowledged; it is not retained as a
build dependency or falsely described as never having existed.

The output directory must be new. Native tool binaries and firmware exports
are distributed separately from the own-format source tree. The compiler CLI
and its lib companions can be relocated together. Use zero INPUT OUTPUT or
ezero INPUT OUTPUT in the corresponding native toolkit. See tools/build.ztext,
tools/compiler-cli.ztext and checks/README.ztext for exact interfaces.
The complete source package is zero-source-0.13.zpack. It excludes private
user disks and historical screenshots. Native toolkit packages are also
.zpack; a standalone zero-archive host executable reads them. For Linux,
grant the downloaded standalone executable permission to run in the host
file manager before opening it. Archive extraction never runs its contents.

Boot and terminal

The same kernel source builds for 32-bit and 64-bit x86 PCs. BIOS selects the
appropriate CPU mode; UEFI has separate IA32 and AMD64 entry points. Eight-bit
CPUs are outside the target. The ISO is optical boot media, not a hybrid USB
disk. Persistent data needs a separate writable disk kept across restarts.

VirtualBox: attach the ISO to an optical drive and select that drive for boot.
Use BIOS or the matching UEFI architecture. A 64-bit guest requires exposed
64-bit CPU support; the BIOS path can instead run the 32-bit kernel. Current
four-path automated boot checks use external emulators; those checks do not
constitute a new native VirtualBox or physical-hardware test.

Terminal file actions include watch, search, read, create, save, del and GO.
write prints text. Volumes have independent letter roots such as A:| and B:|;
GO A:| selects the volume. Paths use a vertical bar. Listings and visible
identifiers start at one. format ZERO initializes a selected disposable disk;
it is not needed on an already initialized disk and destroys its prior format.

Zero and Ezero file operations are real disk operations. create preserves an
existing file, save creates or replaces, writefile creates exclusively, and
del deletes. watch and search return only regular file names. Searches support
* and ?; native ? matches one Unicode scalar. GO/go changes the selected root
or host working directory. disks lists available roots. Read failures and
undersized listing buffers are reported without a partial success result.

ZeroFS currently has eight file slots, 31-byte ASCII names and 400-byte data
per file. The new guest service accepts printable ASCII plus TAB, LF and CR.
ext4, NTFS and Btrfs drivers support only the bounded profiles documented in
fs/*.ztext; unsupported or unsafe volumes are rejected for writes. Native
Linux/Windows operations support larger files and UTF-8 paths. See LANGUAGE.ztext
and ezero/LANGUAGE.ztext rather than assuming native and guest limits match.

Browser and graphical source editor

browser [HTTP_ADDRESS] opens the original Zero page reader in a desktop window.
O edits the address, B goes back, R reloads, Up/Down scroll, Space or Tab selects
a numbered link, Enter follows it, and Esc returns to the terminal. It handles
bounded HTML or plain text, UTF-8 validation, entities, headings/lists/links,
relative addresses and redirects. The original ASCII desktop font substitutes
unavailable glyphs. HTTP framing supports Content-Length and chunked transfer.
The raw response is limited to 65535 bytes and rendered text to 32768 bytes.
The page document uses our ZBROW001 format. No website input becomes a shell
command. This is a text-oriented first browser, not a Chromium-equivalent:
HTTPS/TLS, CSS layout, JavaScript/DOM, forms, multimedia and general modern
website compatibility are not implemented. Chromium code was not copied.

edit [NAME] opens Zero Studio in Zero OS. It provides insertion/deletion,
arrows and a text caret, line numbers, open/create/save, language selection,
and actual Zero/Ezero compilation and execution with output or failure status.
F6 saves, F7 opens, F8 builds and runs, F9 changes language, F10 creates a new
buffer. Unsaved changes require an explicit discard. This version keeps the
existing guest filesystem limit: 400 ASCII bytes per source file. A polled
PS/2 mouse driver supplies an actual moving pointer with background restoration.

zero-studio and zero-studio.exe provide a native graphical editor on Linux x64
and Windows x64. They use original Zero pixel drawing and editing code. The
Linux adapter speaks X11 directly and needs X11 or Xwayland; the Windows
adapter calls documented Win32 interfaces. No foreign GUI toolkit or source
is included. The native editor holds up to 65535 ASCII bytes, supports mouse
caret positioning and safe file replacement, and builds Zero/Ezero with F5
using the original compiler companions in lib/. Keep that directory beside
the executable. See editor/host/README.ztext for keys and exact limits.

What is implemented

The monolithic kernel includes disk drivers, persistent files, terminal,
pixel framebuffer graphics, tiled/scrolling terminal windows, keyboard-driven
system/storage/network applications, and cooperative tasks with independent
stacks. Native Zero and Ezero compilation and execution are available in the
guest. Tasks and applications currently share memory and one CPU.

Wired AMD PCnet support includes DHCP, bounded DNS/TCP/HTTP and connection
status. Wi-Fi has selection/password interface states, but no working physical
Wi-Fi radio driver is claimed. TLS, full modern-web support, SMP, preemptive scheduling,
process isolation, general multimedia playback and a full automatic installer
remain unfinished. The local update tool stages and verifies releases; it is
not an authenticated Internet rolling-update service.

Verification

verification.ztext records this version's completed checks and their limits.
The tests exercise ordinary use, errors, boundaries, data preservation and
cold restarts. Published results must correspond to the exact tested files.
New failures are fixed before artifacts are described as verified.

Zero Media 0.14 addition - 2026-11-20

Own .zmedia disk-image format and Zero Flash tools are implemented in media/.
See media/README.ztext for Linux/Windows commands, firmware-mode Zero use,
source reconstruction and device restrictions. See media/VERIFICATION.ztext
for completed tests and explicit limits. The new disk boots BIOS/UEFI32/64;
the UEFI menu also starts the original Zero flash writer. The kernel's USB
runtime driver is not implemented by this addition. The existing ISO is
retained as a compatibility export; .zmedia is a different binary format.


ZERO WEB0.15PREVIEW ADDITION -25NOVEMBER2026

browser/engine now contains an original Zero HTML tree, CSS cascade, bounded
JavaScript interpreter and native Linux/Windows GUI for local documents.
The new zero-web program accepts HTML or ZTEXT001 and renders its own pixels;
zero-web --render INPUT NEW.zpix exports the original picture format.
Run zero-web browser/engine/demo.ztext for the included demonstration.
This is a preview profile, not a complete Chromium rewrite or Internet browser.
The existing Zero OS HTTP reader remains unchanged; the new engine is not yet
installed as a guest application. No client Python interpreter is included.
Read browser/engine/README.ztext and VERIFICATION.ztext with zero-text.
The builder now emits72artifacts in76stages. All unchanged boot/guest artifacts
are byte-identical to0.14; no new boot/hardware validation is implied.
